From k384 (2026-10-31). On failure ocp status --json and ocp health --json put the line into an error field of the JSON they print; ocp node ls --json prints nothing on STDOUT and only the line on STDERR. A machine reader of --json cannot tell "no nodes" from "could not ask". Decide (here, not silently): node ls --json prints {"error": "..."} with exit 1 like status/health. Done = the same shape for the three, tested. Lane: ocp-worker (no lane owns node ls). After k375 and the non-599 card.

After k387: node ls's list calls go through cluster_api_call (Node/Ls.pm), which dies with the one line. For --json, catch that and print the object with an error field like status/health --json do.

2026-10-31 17:02 Shape decision (k388): the error object REPLACES the array, it does not wrap it. node ls --json prints {"error": "<message>"} (canonical, pretty) on STDOUT, exit 1; STDERR keeps the line exactly as without --json ("Error: ..." from OCP.pm: the error is printed, then re-died). Why: the success contract is a JSON array (POD JSON OUTPUT, xt/smoke-worker.pl pick reads it); turning it into {nodes:[...]} to make room for error would break every reader for the sake of the failure case. Status/health add error to their own success object; node ls success is an array, which has no place for a key, so the type tells them apart: array = inventory ([] = no OCPNodes), object = could not list. No "nodes": [] beside error: health keeps pods: [] because pods is a key of its success object; node ls has none. Caught: everything the --json listing dies with, as health does (API lines of reach_cluster_api/cluster_api_call for the OCPNode AND the Node list; also no ocp.yaml / no kubeconfig). error = the message chomped, as health. OCP::Role::Cmd helpers unchanged. Claims replaced: t/384 + t/387 asserted node ls --json prints nothing on STDOUT; now the object, line as its error, nothing else.

